      "message": "I think there is an opportunity to further simplify this, specifically by removing the global `g_device_removed_flag`. Here\u0027s some code generated by Gemini. Review it and make sure that it works:\n\n----\n\nWhen a USB device resets or disconnects, the operating system tears down the device node. Any subsequent attempt to communicate using your existing `libusb_device_handle` will immediately fail with a `LIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE` code. We can exploit this to detect the reset without any global context, callbacks, or atomic flags.\n\nHere is how to strip out all the hotplug complexity and implement a much simpler polling mechanism.\n\n### 1\\. Simplify `UsbDevice::ResetComplete`\n\nInstead of pumping an event loop and checking flags, simply attempt a benign control transfer (like requesting the active configuration). If it fails because the device is gone, the reset is complete.\n\nReplace the current implementation in `usb_context.cc` with this:\n\n```c\nint UsbDevice::ResetComplete(int timeout_sec) const {\n  if (!handle_) {\n    LOGE(\"Invalid device handle\");\n    return -1;\n  }\n\n  const int poll_ms \u003d 100;\n  const int timeout_ms \u003d timeout_sec * 1000;\n  int elapsed_ms \u003d 0;\n\n  while (timeout_sec \u003d\u003d 0 || elapsed_ms \u003c timeout_ms) {\n    int config \u003d 0;\n    // Attempt a harmless control transfer to the device\n    int r \u003d libusb_get_configuration(handle_.get(), \u0026config);\n\n    // If the OS dropped the device due to the MCU reset, the handle \n    // becomes stale and libusb throws this specific error.\n    if (r \u003d\u003d LIBUSB_ERROR_NO_DEVICE) {\n      LOGI(\"USB device removal/reset detected\");\n      return 0; // Success\n    }\n\n    std::this_thread::sleep_for(std::chrono::milliseconds(poll_ms));\n    elapsed_ms +\u003d poll_ms;\n  }\n\n  LOGW(\"Timeout after {} seconds waiting for device removal\", timeout_sec);\n  return -1;\n}\n\n```\n\n### 2\\. Gut the Hotplug Boilerplate\n\nBecause we are now relying on standard I/O errors, you can delete massive chunks of code from your implementation:\n\n* **Delete the callback:** Completely remove the `UsbRemoveEventCallback` function from `usb_context.cc`.  \n* **Remove registration:** In `UsbDevice::Create`, delete the entire `if (libusb_has_capability(LIBUSB_CAP_HAS_HOTPLUG)) { ... }` block. You no longer need to register or deregister anything.  \n* **Clean up the Header:** Remove all the hotplug-related cruft from `usb_context.h`. Your class definition becomes much cleaner:\n\n```c\nclass UsbDevice {\n public:\n  static std::expected\u003cUsbDevice, FtError\u003e Create(\n      const UsbContext\u0026 ctx, UsbConnectionDetails\u0026 out_details);\n\n  std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Send(std::span\u003cconst uint8_t\u003e buf,\n                                   uint32_t timeout_ms) const;\n  std::expected\u003cint, FtError\u003e Recv(std::span\u003cuint8_t\u003e buf,\n                                   uint32_t timeout_ms) const;\n\n  libusb_device_handle* get() const { return handle_.get(); }\n  \n  void ReleaseInterface(int interface_num) {\n    if (handle_) {\n      libusb_release_interface(handle_.get(), interface_num);\n    }\n  }\n\n  int ResetComplete(int timeout_sec) const;\n\n private:\n  UsbDevice(libusb_device_handle* handle, uint8_t ep_in, uint8_t ep_out)\n      : handle_(handle),\n        endpoint_in_(ep_in),\n        endpoint_out_(ep_out) {}\n\n  std::unique_ptr\u003clibusb_device_handle, LibusbDeviceHandleDeleter\u003e handle_;\n  uint8_t endpoint_in_ \u003d 0;\n  uint8_t endpoint_out_ \u003d 0;\n};\n\n```\n\n### Why This is Better\n\n1. **Zero Global State:** You no longer need `std::atomic` or shared pointers.  \n2. **No Memory Leaks:** You don\u0027t have to worry about writing a custom destructor to carefully unregister `libusb_hotplug_callback_handle`s to prevent segmentation faults.  \n3. **Strictly Sequential:** The code flow matches the mental model of a CLI tool (send command \\-\\\u003e poll for disconnect \\-\\\u003e exit).",
